course: dictionary
lesson: 1959
title: Comparing things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To compare means to look at two or more things to see how they are the same and how they are different. You can also compare one thing to another to say it is like it. Wordsworth did this when he wrote «I wandered lonely as a cloud», comparing himself to a cloud drifting along all alone.
explain: The words «compared with» or «compared to» mean you set one thing beside the other. In a test, a new medicine is «compared with a dummy pill» so that scientists can see whether it really works and is safe.
explain: In maths, to compare two numbers, we ask how many more one has than the other. We can also use the signs >, < and = to show which is bigger, smaller or the same.
fact: Compare means to look at two or more things to see how they are alike and different.
fact: «Compared with» or «compared to» means setting one thing beside another.
fact: A simile compares two things using «as» or «like», as in «as lonely as a cloud».
fact: Scientists compare results, and careful notes let other people check and compare them.
fact: Charles Darwin filled notebooks on his voyage on the ship Beagle, and his big idea was published in 1859.
exercise: When we compare two numbers in maths, what do we ask? | How old the numbers are | *How many more one has than the other | What colour the numbers are
exercise: «I wandered lonely as a cloud.» Which word shows that Wordsworth is making a comparison? | wandered | lonely | *as | cloud
exercise: Mum wants to save money on shopping. What should she do? | Pay for everything without looking | Buy the first thing she sees | Ignore the price labels | *Compare the prices of the same items in two shops
test: «A new medicine is compared with a dummy pill before it is sold.» Why is it compared? | *To make sure it really works and is safe | To make it taste nicer | To find out what colour it should be | To make it cheaper to sell
test: What does «compared to» mean? | Losing count of the items | Running away from a problem | *Setting one thing beside another | Changing one thing into gold
exam: Why do careful notes matter to scientists? | So they never need to look at their work again | *So other people can check and compare | So the notebooks get heavier | So they can hide their results
